{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi","id":858849,"title":"My problem with the Nephandi","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-04T03:01:55-07:00","id":858849,"title":"My problem with the Nephandi","content":"<span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Almost finished the Mage 20, and been loving almost all of it. The idea of so many everyday actions - such as putting on perfume before a meeting - being actual rituals, is fantastic.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">ALMOST all of it. Do you think there are many humans who simultaneously have all the following qualities:</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">1) They are suicidal, seeking their own destruction and that of the world.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">2) They think over the long term.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">3) They are highly disciplined, discrete, and good at hiding their own personality.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">4) They are immune to social pressure from those they interact with day after day after day.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">5) They are wantonly sadistic and enjoy the suffering of others.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">6) They coordinate perfectly with each other, and have an efficient hierarchy that disseminates information and instructions while leaving minimal traces. This means they have minimal infighting.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">7) They are mages, so filled with hubris and arrogance.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">And so, the Nephandi fail for me. Big time. Finding one suicidal-planner-discreet-disciplined-sadistic-bureaucratic-hubristic person might be possible, or a small group, but a whole faction? We should believe that, alone of any large groups of humans throughout history, ones who have solved the whole &quot;efficient organisation with minimal overhead&quot; problem are the self-destructive devil worshippers? If you think of them as human beings with their own twisted wants and agendas, ask yourself &quot;what makes them work together better than any other group&quot;?</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Basically, we've never seen anything like this in human history, and the Nephandi are supposed to be able to keep it up for millennia. It's beyond ridiculous. And if the Nephandi had that level of organisational coherence, there would be no need for infiltration, they could take down the others in quasi-open battle.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">And adding them as infiltrators only seems to lessen the interest of corruption or amorality in the other organisations.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Technocracy reignites the pogrom! Is this the moral thing to do? How are the leaders grappling with the difficult decisions of... oh wait, it's just Nephandi infiltration.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">The traditions fall apart and some start really exploiting the sleepers! Is this the inevitable tensions in an organisation on the brink? Where does expediency start and morality end? Should we... oh wait, it's just Nephandi infiltration.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">I think you get my point. Now, *accusations* of Nephandi influence are perfectly fine, or the occasional Mage going to far into demeonology. Humans accusing other humans of being demon worshippers is credible and likely, as is some turning to immorality for an extra edge. But for me, as an organised group, they achieve the rare double feat of being simultaneously unbelievable and uninteresting.</span></span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4851,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4851-cheesefondue","user":"Cheesefondue","date":". 04-04-2016, 09:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4851-cheesefondue","name":"Cheesefondue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"275"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T03:54:59-07:00","id":858869,"title":"","content":"I <i>think</i> the idea is that they're not so much &quot;people&quot; as meat puppets of a vast demonic intelligence, so that 1), 5) and 7) are the criteria for &quot;joining&quot; and everything else is the forces they've given themselves to, moving them like pieces on a board. But I don't actually play Mage, so I could be way off base.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2082-schleiermacher","name":"Schleiermacher","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T03:57:47-07:00","id":858871,"title":"","content":"Well, the whole idea behind them was an orgy of debased Nihilism. The one thing that the book doesn't make clear is how rare the Nephandi actually are. Inferalism and corruption is easy... everyone could make the wrong deal, but the Nephandi are way beyond that that. The game justifies this with them literally being utterly insane and performing bizzare rites that invert their avatars and turn them into zealous masters of debasement. Now, it does help if you consider there are quite a few different Nephandic factions with different party lines, and that the whole &quot;Nephandi are dark and insidious&quot; is counterbalanced by insane gonzo murder maniacs - like say Tezghul the insane.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, we've actually been discussing this whole infiltration thing for a while now. Infiltration used to be a small thing - it was a way to build paranoia and create chaos and mayhem within the Mage groups. However, never before has the concept of Nephandic corruption being so thorough been a thing in Mage. M20 pretty much states that the Technocracy is a lost cause because it's actually ran by the Nephandi, and here you're utterly correct in your statement that it makes the whole thing bullshit.<br /> <br /> They can work quite well, but they haven't really been represented properly in M20.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T04:47:39-07:00","id":858889,"title":"","content":"It's going back to the Second Edition theme of everything being run by a conspiracy behind a conspiracy. But the current developer *was* Mage 2e, so...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T06:16:49-07:00","id":858940,"title":"","content":"I actually consider the whole &quot;Nephandi are basically utter, dark representatives of <i>Descension</i>! So dark and horrid that imagine that if you connect to them with Mind...well you are already on your way to becoming one of THEM!&quot; to be Tradition, Disparates and Technocracy propaganda.<br /> <br /> Yes, there are Mages who - you could say - descend into an apocalyptic paradigm. And many of them are among those that were seduced by say, Jodi Blake (who is, I think, credited with popularizing Cauls, or something? Possibly more Tradition propaganda directed at the possibly quite likeable Celestial Choir runaway). But vast majority of what's called Infernal Nephandi are not actually apocalyptic in their desires. (And then the book's lines says: &quot;But they are into Descension!&quot;...to which I answer: &quot;Yeah, and so are Euthanatos or Ba'ta who invoke Baron Samedi, perhaps.&quot;) The Demon Lords desire power, yes.<br /> And they would enslave the world given the chance. Maybe..who knows? (Many of Demonic Patrons described in Book of Madness come out actually being reasonable - as in, not strictly evil; more of ruthless embodiment/representatives of the ideas that they represent.) But at the same time, I'd say that even a wholly evil Demon Lord would be quite realistic in their attitude and relations with True Mages that serve them..so they wouldn't give them constant orders of &quot;Bring about the apocalypse in few hundred years.&quot; because they know they would be stopped. Or they don't really care about taking over the world <i>that much</i>...or whatever.<br /> (not fully that sure about what I wrote here about Demon Lords, but what I mean is that Infernal Nephandi are not Black Spiral Dancer level of evil)<br /> <br /> Basically, the other Mages are discriminating against anyone who: has 'Pacts', deals with Deep Umbral entities, deals with Demons, and who has Wyrm taint on him, has been to Black Spiral, has been to a Caul, hangs out with Mages who are 'Nephandi'.<br /> <br /> I don't have the time or really the knowledge to consider what the books calls the K'llashaa. But I think those Mages who went to Black Spiral or the WtA Malfeas are, most likely, quite unsalvageable. But they are still human, and they are not the same Nephandi who go into the Cauls!!! (the fleshy sacs that condition the Nephandi for loyalty and do horrible things to their avatar and/or souls. It is described directly in Book of Shadows, pages 81-83.) Or are they the same? Does going into a Caul make someone a K'llashaa? Anyone knows concrete info on this conundrum?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Schleiermacher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=858869#post858869\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I <i>think</i> the idea is that they're not so much &quot;people&quot; as meat puppets of a vast demonic intelligence, so that 1), 5) and 7) are the criteria for &quot;joining&quot; and everything else is the forces they've given themselves to, moving them like pieces on a board. But I don't actually play Mage, so I could be way off base.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, that seems to be the idea that the books are going - all across the board - with Nephandi. But it's just soooo...boring. I much prefer Nephandi as a proper Black Mage archetype. Varied, possibly understandable, maybe even sometimes agreeable or redeemable! (or along the lines of |necessary evil| )<br /> The books just seem to be so strong with the fact that once you become even an Arete 1-3 Nephandi...it's already toooo late for you. Or something...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3254,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","user":"Muad'Dib","date":". 05-09-2016, 06:39 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T06:46:26-07:00","id":858960,"title":"","content":"K'llashaa are essentially lovecraftian terrors from beyond space-time. All tentacles, chaos, insanity and primordial power. Their Nephandi would essentially be like the servants of Azhatoth or Nyarlathotep. You've also skipped the faction that simply adores the Oblivion.<br /> <br /> And there is more then having a &quot;pact&quot;. The difference is that say the Dreamspeakers and the Hermetics make pacts as equal trades of services. While Demonic pacts are all elaborate cons that hook into your soul and parcel it out for some other entities abuse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T07:39:41-07:00","id":858994,"title":"","content":"I enjoyed the depiction of Jody Blake in the Penny Dreadful novel. The only thing that sets her apart from Penny is that she made a deal with a demon. She even considers Penny as part of the same &quot;sisterhood&quot; and Penny would readily agree that both of them are witches.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11524-the_1of3","name":"The_1of3","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T08:15:04-07:00","id":859013,"title":"","content":"The Nephandi chapter, because it is meant almost exclusively for NPCs, is riddled with propaganda and fearful story hooks. There's no need to present the great misunderstandings of the past the way the Tradition and Technocracy chapters did, because the Nephandi aren't running from their past. There's no need to point out where their master plan is weak because there is no master plan. The result is that they look like powerful, perfect infiltrators who are always lurking around every corner.<br /> <br /> And I have to say that the chapter really bugs me for precisely that reason. The Nephandi aren't special. They aren't better than anyone else in Mage, they are prone to the same hubris and the same mistakes. So it really drives me crazy that the chapter often implies they are just so much more successful.<br /> <br /> However, there are two things to remember that mitigate some of the problems.<br /> <br /> The first is that the Nephandi are not a united front. So to revisit your list, you don't find these traits in all Nephandi:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cheesefondue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi#post858849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Almost finished the Mage 20, and been loving almost all of it. The idea of so many everyday actions - such as putting on perfume before a meeting - being actual rituals, is fantastic.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">ALMOST all of it. Do you think there are many humans who simultaneously have all the following qualities:</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">1) They are suicidal, seeking their own destruction and that of the world.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">2) They think over the long term.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">3) They are highly disciplined, discrete, and good at hiding their own personality.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">4) They are immune to social pressure from those they interact with day after day after day.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">5) They are wantonly sadistic and enjoy the suffering of others.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">6) They coordinate perfectly with each other, and have an efficient hierarchy that disseminates information and instructions while leaving minimal traces. This means they have minimal infighting.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#141823\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">7) They are mages, so filled with hubris and arrogance.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1) is always true, and 4) and 5) are mostly true, simply because the Nephandi are fundamentally and metaphysically altered by the Cauls. It's not that they are uniform or incapable of changing their minds, it's that their minds and souls have an inevitable gravity towards those (self) destructive impulses.<br /> <br /> 2) and 3) are traits they can have, but are actually not all that common.<br /> <br /> 6) is just wrong.<br /> <br /> They can cooperate, but can also gleefully feed each other into the woodchipper. In fact I think there are more than a few lines in the chapter that imply their infiltration has more to do with sacrificing their pawns and scapegoating than to actual mastery at fitting in. There's really nothing a Nephandus won't destroy, including herself if it pushes her agenda.<br /> <br /> 7) is the real problem. The chapter really presents the Nephandi like they are the only ones who know what is going on. They are not only better and more honest than everyone else, they are the inevitable winners because the world is so corrupt they barely have to push to get it to topple. Everything is rapidly falling apart anyways, its just a stubborn refusal to admit it that keeps people going. A paper thin rationalization that's been waiting for someone to tear it apart...<br /> <br /> For the last 10,000 years...<br /> <br /> The second and most important thing to remember about the Nephandi chapter is that it is a lie. It's the World of Darkness, not as it is but as the Nephandi often see it. You are getting a little piece of the madness that makes them what they are. Far from lacking hubris, the chapter is thick with it. They are certain of their victory. They are certain of their rightness. There is no room for the possibility of failure, because how could that even happen?<br /> <br /> And I'm sure they thought the same thing before their defeat at the end of WW2.<br /> <br /> Are the Nephandi poised to start some serious shit? Sure. Are they the strongest faction? The best infiltrators? The ones about to win the entire pot? That's remains to be seen.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T09:29:46-07:00","id":859067,"title":"","content":"And there is an M20 Stretch goal book we're supposed to see sometime. It's not even made it to the top of the list yet. But it's supposed to, eventually, be coming. After they actually finish the core book with the gaping rules hole for item creation and lots of other little bits and pieces (two different books). <br /> <br /> So MAYBE, we'll get a book about the Nephandi and using them, but it's likely to be more of the same: They rule the world and will win in the end (Unless the Disparates can stop them! Go Team No-Way-They-Can-Even-Have-A-Sit-Down (without it turning into a shouting match cum bloodbath)!) The Technocracy has been theirs from the very beginning. They've made the Traditions their bitch by making them bitchy and crabby. Etc. Etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T16:34:14-07:00","id":859321,"title":"","content":"I think a lot about the Nephandi ability to be &quot;successful&quot;, despite having low numbers relative to other groups (at least in terms of Awakened Mages; this is not even counting the Sorcerers, Infernalists, cultists, and dupes the Nephandi surround themselves with, secretly direct, and sacrifice like pawns), is the fact that the Nephandi have a different Win Condition. They seek to serve their unholy masters (in whatever flavor sparks the individual Nephandus' fancy) and doom the world. They aren't fighting over the same resources, territories, and public support as the Traditions and Technocracy. They aren't trying to change the hearts and minds of Sleepers (beyond inducing them towards depravity and sin). If any one Nephandi cell - any individual plotter - manages to tear a hole in the world and let their masters in, they've won.<br /> <br /> The Nephandi don't need to dominate the world. Just spoil it for everyone else. In that sense, they are fighting a different battle than all the other factions, on a battlefield that favors them. And they can keep throwing cultists, unwitting patsies, and their own reincarnations at the problem until they achieve their goals.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T18:05:19-07:00","id":859357,"title":"","content":"Apparently, the world isn't really all that fragile though... So, great, you have a winning condition that you don't need cooperation for, but it doesn't look like it's any easier than &quot;Rule the World&quot; or &quot;Mass Ascension&quot;. Based on the reasoning being used, the Traditions should have the easiest go of it. All each individual Tradition mage has to do is keep everyone else at bay long enough for each one of them to personally Ascend.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T20:09:17-07:00","id":859416,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=859357#post859357\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Apparently, the world isn't really all that fragile though... So, great, you have a winning condition that you don't need cooperation for, but it doesn't look like it's any easier than &quot;Rule the World&quot; or &quot;Mass Ascension&quot;. Based on the reasoning being used, the Traditions should have the easiest go of it. All each individual Tradition mage has to do is keep everyone else at bay long enough for each one of them to personally Ascend.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, because it's really that easy for someone - anyone - to Ascend, let alone their entire membership.<br /> <br /> The Traditions also have more than just personal Ascension as their goal. If that was it, they'd all retreat to Horizon realms and let the Technocracy have the world. What the Traditions want is to make their brand(s) of magick work on Earth again. They don't want to live in hiding, too afraid of Paradox and the watchful eyes of the Technocratic Union to perform anything but the most coincidental spells. Mages have the potential to do and be anything, yet are held back. They can't accept defeat, or surrender, or obscurity. They want to take back the world.<br /> <br /> Moreover, individual Traditions have moral and philosophical reasons for wanting to spread their Paradigm. The Celestial Chorus has its various religions, and want to combat the godlessness and sin present in the world. The Dreamspeakers have their various tribes, etc, that must be defended from an imperialistic mono-culture, one that would happily either destroy the ethnic groups or destroy their cultural identity. The Euthanatos see the Wheel in imbalance, and desire to serve its proper turning; healing or reforming those who can be saved, and giving the Good Death to those who cannot. Etc, etc.<br /> <br /> Not to mention bloody-minded pride and deep-seated animosity. The Traditions have been fighting too hard, for too long, to let the battle end with anything but victory.<br /> <br /> What this means is that the Traditions don't have the option for such a pat, easy Win Condition. They want many things, and the Technocracy is directly in competition for those things. Power in many forms, resources, newly Awakened Mages to mold, the hearts and minds of the common man, the ability to dictate what is right and wrong, and many more things besides. These are battle grounds on which the Traditions and the Technocracy (and the Disparates) compete. They have Win Conditions that are incompatible, but only because they are finite.<br /> <br /> The Nephandi do not share those as Win Conditions. They don't want to control anything...they just want to break everything. And it's far easier to break something than it is to build. Building is what the other sects want, while Breaking is what the Nephandi want. In this, they have the flexibility to choose their battles and battlefields. While the Traditions must react if the Technocracy tries to claim ground in the former's sphere of influence (and vice versa), the Nephandi can attack in ways and at times no one is expecting, for purposes that no other rational actors would consider. It is for this reason that Nephandi rarely engage in &quot;open&quot; conflict with other sects: why fight the enemy on the field when you can undermine them from within? Why fight over a well when you can poison it instead?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T06:28:48-07:00","id":859589,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=859416#post859416\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Nephandi do not share those as Win Conditions. They don't want to control anything...they just want to break everything. And it's far easier to break something than it is to build. Building is what the other sects want, while Breaking is what the Nephandi want. In this, they have the flexibility to choose their battles and battlefields. While the Traditions must react if the Technocracy tries to claim ground in the former's sphere of influence (and vice versa), the Nephandi can attack in ways and at times no one is expecting, for purposes that no other rational actors would consider. It is for this reason that Nephandi rarely engage in &quot;open&quot; conflict with other sects: why fight the enemy on the field when you can undermine them from within? Why fight over a well when you can poison it instead?</div> </div> </div> </div>And here we reach the biggest problem with the Nephandic kind of win condition. Claiming every piece of destruction or degradation as a step towards the final Absolute sounds good, but it is empty propaganda. It denies the actual challenge in front of them.<br /> <br /> Sure, turning a building into a pile of rubble is easy, but keeping it a pile of rubble is next to impossible. People rebuild, they learn, they grow. Every small victory is wiped away as humanity rises again, often to even greater heights. Win or lose, as long as the board exists sleepers and mages will play on it. The Nephandi line about the world being corrupt and dying? It's not true. Not yet.<br /> <br /> That's why the real Nephandi struggle is always about changing the game itself. Summoning abominations from beyond to reshape the world, desecrating the very nature of humanity, and infiltrating and corrupting the other players aren't the easiest paths to victory, they are the only paths to victory. If it sounds like the Nephandi are pursuing Ascension level struggles, it is because that is exactly what they are trying to accomplish: Mass Descension. The world isn't what they say it is, so they have to change it. <br /> <br /> Far from having the easiest win conditions, they have the hardest. Destruction is easy, making it mean something is the difficult part.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T06:51:34-07:00","id":859603,"title":"","content":"Cheesefondue - there are two main things that make the Nephandi in general believable to me (setting aside the M20 &quot;they're everywhere!&quot; thing).<br /> <br /> First - remember the Cauls. They alter your soul so that destruction and decay is <i>beautiful</i> to you. Think of the thing that elevates your mind - fine art, exquisite wine, the love of a loved one; that's the feeling a Nephandi gets when they break someone down, when they harm. They're &quot;immune to social pressure&quot; in the same way that you don't stop loving <i>Starry Night</i> just because your circle of friends don't like art. They're &quot;wantonly sadistic&quot; in the same way that a person who loves amazing wine is likely to be subscribed to <i>Wine Spectator</i> and have an excellent wine cellar - it's the natural outcome of the internal change. The evil of the Nephandi is startlingly natural once they've got the inverted Avatar.<br /> <br /> Second - also about the Cauls - the inversion cannot be undone. Every time they corrupt another, deeply and truly in the Cauls, they permanently gain an ally, even after that Avatar's current host dies. All the Traditions and Technocracy can do is cut off the damage with Gilgul, and that's if they're lucky. In the truly long term, the Nephandi always win the numbers game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T08:53:34-07:00","id":859676,"title":"","content":"The first mage I ever played was an Orphan who hooked up with the party because he didn't have any other potential mentors. He was...a bit of the tag-along kid, though his Avatar manifested as his best friend (who kind-of looked like his twin sister, which he didn't have). She was an imp, personality-wise, and he was a punk teen with a burning desire to be taken seriously. Things went bad in an encounter in a Nephandus sanctum, and he died.<br /> <br /> Except...he didn't. His Avatar saved him, guiding him back to the land of the living, a little confused and worse for the wear, but...alive.<br /> <br /> At least, that's what he <i>thought </i>had happened. In reality, in his fear and terror, he made a terrible mistake and allowed some sort of Nephandus spirit to consume and replace his Avatar; he wasn't nihilistic nor self-destructive (at least, not on purpose), but when one of the lessons he HAD learned was that he's supposed to listen to his Avatar on his path to enlightenment, he suddenly had a very bad influence in his life. And he was, technically, a Nephandus himself, since his Avatar had been...replaced. It's possible, if the game had gone on longer, he might have really twisted towards evil, but as it was, he mostly didn't understand what he was doing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T09:55:22-07:00","id":859710,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=859416#post859416\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, because it's really that easy for someone - anyone - to Ascend, let alone their entire membership.<br /> <br /> The Traditions also have more than just personal Ascension as their goal. If that was it, they'd all retreat to Horizon realms and let the Technocracy have the world. What the Traditions want is to make their brand(s) of magick work on Earth again. They don't want to live in hiding, too afraid of Paradox and the watchful eyes of the Technocratic Union to perform anything but the most coincidental spells. Mages have the potential to do and be anything, yet are held back. They can't accept defeat, or surrender, or obscurity. They want to take back the world.<br /> <br /> Moreover, individual Traditions have moral and philosophical reasons for wanting to spread their Paradigm. The Celestial Chorus has its various religions, and want to combat the godlessness and sin present in the world. The Dreamspeakers have their various tribes, etc, that must be defended from an imperialistic mono-culture, one that would happily either destroy the ethnic groups or destroy their cultural identity. The Euthanatos see the Wheel in imbalance, and desire to serve its proper turning; healing or reforming those who can be saved, and giving the Good Death to those who cannot. Etc, etc.<br /> <br /> Not to mention bloody-minded pride and deep-seated animosity. The Traditions have been fighting too hard, for too long, to let the battle end with anything but victory.<br /> <br /> What this means is that the Traditions don't have the option for such a pat, easy Win Condition. They want many things, and the Technocracy is directly in competition for those things. Power in many forms, resources, newly Awakened Mages to mold, the hearts and minds of the common man, the ability to dictate what is right and wrong, and many more things besides. These are battle grounds on which the Traditions and the Technocracy (and the Disparates) compete. They have Win Conditions that are incompatible, but only because they are finite.<br /> <br /> The Nephandi do not share those as Win Conditions. They don't want to control anything...they just want to break everything. And it's far easier to break something than it is to build. Building is what the other sects want, while Breaking is what the Nephandi want. In this, they have the flexibility to choose their battles and battlefields. While the Traditions must react if the Technocracy tries to claim ground in the former's sphere of influence (and vice versa), the Nephandi can attack in ways and at times no one is expecting, for purposes that no other rational actors would consider. It is for this reason that Nephandi rarely engage in &quot;open&quot; conflict with other sects: why fight the enemy on the field when you can undermine them from within? Why fight over a well when you can poison it instead?</div> </div> </div> </div>Saracasm aside. Yes. It is. MUCH easier to Ascend than to destroy the universe by poking a hole in it (a <i>beautiful</i>, <i>magnificent</i>, <i>wonderfully decadent </i>hole of <b>decay </b>and <b>destruction</b>... because, ya know, the Cauls...) How do we know that? Because people HAVE Ascended (unless they haven't, but the general trend is towards that and away from the &quot;Big Lie&quot; concept). And the Universe hasn't been destroyed. Ergo, one is easier than the other. All other objectives of the Traditions aside, if they can serve as Ascension incubators (and they do) for individual mages to go from Awakened caterpillars to Ascended butterflies, then they refute the &quot;Nephandi have the easiest road&quot; hypothesis. In fact, It seems like the Nephandi's goal is more of a chore than the Union's Mass Ascension, due to the previously described lack of any semblance of internal organization and goal-oriented cooperation, which the Technocracy has in theory (completely) and functionally (not as much, but it's definitely there). (Unless of course, the whole Union is a front for some aberrant Nephandi plot that has held together for centuries despite the whole tendency to tear stuff apart, the Nephandi raise d'etre after all.)<br /> <br /> In fact, you have a 0th premise in that hypothesis as is. That destroying is easier than creating. It's definitely something we, in our culture, have internalized (up to and including how the zeitgeist interprets thermodynamic entropy), but that's not necessarily the view of others, particularly where magic and worldview is concerned. Evil can just as easily be seen as HARD WORK, because you are consciously going against the natural grain of creation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T15:33:01-07:00","id":859931,"title":"","content":"Even if destruction is easy, you still need a lot of coordination. For instance, if you want to bring down a particular mage... you can assault them, make them go paranoid, spread rumours they are going nephandi, actually turn them nephandi, and so on. Except those plans undermine each other, and without strong coordination, you'd have each nephandi following their own plans and getting in each others way. Same goes for plans targeting organisations; sowing internal distrust and assaulting them from outside are two things you don't want to be doing simultaneously.<br /> <br /> Above all else, it's this incredibly efficient coordination that makes no sense for the nephandi. They should be much more backstabby and treacherous than the traditions, for instance, not the perfect cogs of a well-oiled corruption machine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4851-cheesefondue","name":"Cheesefondue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"275"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T20:39:42-07:00","id":860084,"title":"","content":"Perhaps individual Nephandi have Descended, just as other mages have Ascended. But what would that even look like? That they've merged with Oblivion? That they've become one with the Other that is the antithesis of the One? Or do they forever consign a piece of the One to nothingness?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T01:40:16-07:00","id":860176,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=860084#post860084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps individual Nephandi have Descended, just as other mages have Ascended. But what would that even look like? That they've merged with Oblivion? That they've become one with the Other that is the antithesis of the One? Or do they forever consign a piece of the One to nothingness?</div> </div> </div> </div>All the options are schr\u00f6dinger. If they have really changed their being so substantially, they would not be comprehensible to human beings. In the great scheme of subjective reality, all those things are equally right and wrong. It doesn't matter, whether you ascend for being a particularly good or bad person. Afterwards these things do not matter anymore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11524-the_1of3","name":"The_1of3","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T09:31:27-07:00","id":860372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cheesefondue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=859931#post859931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Above all else, it's this incredibly efficient coordination that makes no sense for the nephandi. They should be much more backstabby and treacherous than the traditions, for instance, not the perfect cogs of a well-oiled corruption machine.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's exactly how I run them. If it weren't for the different factions betraying each other the Fallen would have won long ago. There's a good bit of fiction in one of the books (Book of Madness?) where two Nephandi Masters get into a duel over who gets to corrupt someone, which I always thought was typical Fallen behaviour. In my current story a sect of Outer Things worshippers are scheming to bring down Pentex for example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9496-barbelith","name":"Barbelith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9496_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T12:09:56-07:00","id":860472,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cheesefondue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=859931#post859931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even if destruction is easy, you still need a lot of coordination. For instance, if you want to bring down a particular mage... you can assault them, make them go paranoid, spread rumours they are going nephandi, actually turn them nephandi, and so on. Except those plans undermine each other, and without strong coordination, you'd have each nephandi following their own plans and getting in each others way. Same goes for plans targeting organisations; sowing internal distrust and assaulting them from outside are two things you don't want to be doing simultaneously.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you though? How much do those plans really undermine each other?<br /> <br /> If four different Nephandi, an assaulter, a manipulator, a rumor monger, and a corrupter, all decide that a mage (let's call him Harvey) is in the way of their plans and get to work, what happens? The threat of the assaulter is the perfect fodder for the manipulator and corrupter to get close and go to work. Their plans might not interfere with each other at all, but even if they do one can out the other to gain Harvey's trust. They can even rat each other out, and their plans still go forward. The manipulator revealed gets Harvey jumping at the shadows, just as he wants. The corrupter revealed loses some ground, true, but she's far from the worst threat in Harvey's life, so she's not out of the picture yet. Meanwhile, a Harvey on the run has left himself open for the assaulter to do her work, and the rumor monger is starting to whisper &quot;who is this Harvey that the Nephandi are fighting over him, must be a widderslainte...&quot; And with his friends dwindling, the corrupter's offer is starting to look really appealing.<br /> <br /> Harvey is screwed in any number of ways, likely can't interfere in whatever plans they needed him out of the way for, and the Nephandi didn't even have to talk to each other to do it. In fact they used each other as disposable pawns, which is the opposite of cooperation. At the worst they wasted resources.<br /> <br /> Now, if the various Nephandi needed him for specific reasons, if the assaulter needed to sacrifice him brutally on a specific night and the corrupter needed to turn him Barrabi to have an inside man, then there might be a conflict. One Nephandus will have to go home disappointed. Or brutally murder his rival, I suppose. But how often do you think that happens, comparatively?<br /> <br /> It's the same way for your organizational struggle. The enemy at the gates is the perfect source of tension. The infiltrators can capitalize on that to spread discord and frame potential reformers. Meanwhile the internal struggle ruins coordination and weakens defenses. Both groups benefit, even without any intentional cooperation, possibly without even knowing about each other (as Nephandi I mean, there's no way the infiltrators will miss the enemies at the gate).<br /> <br /> Even if you accept the Nephandi chapter at face value, even if you accept that they are dominating the world, there's no need to presume any kind of incredibly efficient cooperation. The Nephandi have so many irons in the fire that every defeat is the grounds for another victory.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T14:50:32-07:00","id":860567,"title":"","content":"Nephandi are a group of misotehist ( = who hates God) psycopaths who understand the difference between toxic boss and true leader and ideas like Nietzsche's master-slave morality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Master%E2%80%93slave_morality\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Master%E2%80%93slave_morality\" target=\"_blank\"> Master&ndash;slave morality - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Master%E2%80%93slave_morality\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Master%E2%80%93slave_morality </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> They are too selfish to be really loyal to each other but their cults are relatively stables because followers share a common allegiance and they don't notice they are in the evil side.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Tell me what you brag and I will tell you what you lack&quot; (Spanish saying)<br /> <br /> \u00abThe Master Confucius said: \u201cThe noble man is in harmony but does not follow the crowd. The inferior man follows the crowd, but is not in harmony\u201d\u00bb (Anaclet 13:23)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11152-luis-carlos","name":"Luis Carlos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"258"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T15:17:15-07:00","id":860581,"title":"","content":"Considering that the definition of god in Mage, and the fact that a Nephandi can easily be the servant of the judeochristian god and STILL be a nihilistic bastard, I'd hardly define them as god-hating.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T15:44:10-07:00","id":860603,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=859013#post859013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) is always true</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd argue that it isn't, or at least it shouldn't be. It's true for Nephandi that are complete nihilists and want to feed everything to Oblivion. And I suppose Nephandi who serve/worship the Maeljin Incarna want the universe to eventually be negated entirely after it's fully corrupted into a decayed morass, so same goal with different journey to get there. But infernalists serve the will of demons who are pretty clearly invested in continuing to exist, just with themselves in charge of the system rather than being defeated prisoners. And it's a flip of the coin whether the things in the Outer Dark want to be let into creation so they can feed off it and remake it in their own image or just snuff out existence as an affront to their alien sensibilities\u2014their worshippers clearly don't have enough understanding of their masters to definitively say one way or another. I think the attempt to unify all Nephandi under a single uniform and uniformly understood philosophy of nihilistic Descension is a big mistake.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T19:01:21-07:00","id":860711,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=860472#post860472\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Do you though? How much do those plans really undermine each other?<br /> <br /> If four different Nephandi, an assaulter, a manipulator, a rumor monger, and a corrupter, all decide that a mage (let's call him Harvey) is in the way of their plans and get to work, what happens? The threat of the assaulter is the perfect fodder for the manipulator and corrupter to get close and go to work. Their plans might not interfere with each other at all, but even if they do one can out the other to gain Harvey's trust. They can even rat each other out, and their plans still go forward. The manipulator revealed gets Harvey jumping at the shadows, just as he wants. The corrupter revealed loses some ground, true, but she's far from the worst threat in Harvey's life, so she's not out of the picture yet. Meanwhile, a Harvey on the run has left himself open for the assaulter to do her work, and the rumor monger is starting to whisper &quot;who is this Harvey that the Nephandi are fighting over him, must be a widderslainte...&quot; And with his friends dwindling, the corrupter's offer is starting to look really appealing.<br /> <br /> Harvey is screwed in any number of ways, likely can't interfere in whatever plans they needed him out of the way for, and the Nephandi didn't even have to talk to each other to do it. In fact they used each other as disposable pawns, which is the opposite of cooperation. At the worst they wasted resources.<br /> <br /> Now, if the various Nephandi needed him for specific reasons, if the assaulter needed to sacrifice him brutally on a specific night and the corrupter needed to turn him Barrabi to have an inside man, then there might be a conflict. One Nephandus will have to go home disappointed. Or brutally murder his rival, I suppose. But how often do you think that happens, comparatively?<br /> <br /> It's the same way for your organizational struggle. The enemy at the gates is the perfect source of tension. The infiltrators can capitalize on that to spread discord and frame potential reformers. Meanwhile the internal struggle ruins coordination and weakens defenses. Both groups benefit, even without any intentional cooperation, possibly without even knowing about each other (as Nephandi I mean, there's no way the infiltrators will miss the enemies at the gate).<br /> <br /> Even if you accept the Nephandi chapter at face value, even if you accept that they are dominating the world, there's no need to presume any kind of incredibly efficient cooperation. The Nephandi have so many irons in the fire that every defeat is the grounds for another victory.</div> </div> </div> </div>This plan seems to require a ratio of 4:1 Nephandi:target mage to work. As well as a massive series of happy accidents to take the place of actual teamwork. That doesn't seem to argue that Nephandi are effective. It seems to argue that they might, in very rare, circumstances, be able to throw massive resources at a problem and, if everything doesn't go completely wrong, achieve a point where there's a reasonable chance they might be effective. <br /> <br /> Again. &quot;So many irons in the fire&quot; implies LOTS and LOTS of Nephandi. To have all the irons required to have even the appearance of omnipresent plotting verging on imminent success from the Nephandi chapter, there would NEED to be that a high ratio of Nephandi to other mages. <br /> <br /> So, yeah, sure, if there are TONS of Nephandi, they don't need to cooperate to win, they can trip all over each other like the Keystone cops of Damnation and still achieve their objectives, but that leads to a huge set of additional issues. In essence, What Traditions? What Technocracy? What Disparates? The dominant faction of mages, both numerically and in terms of effectiveness is the Nephandi. Which isn't bad for certain values of some types of Mage game, but as the default?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T23:30:28-07:00","id":860822,"title":"","content":"There are two workable models for the Nephandi, IMHO:<br /> <br /> 1) the Nephandi are a dangerous, organized force because their masters organize them. <br /> 2) the Nephandi aren't a dangerous, organized force; <i>individual</i> Nephandi are dangerous to <i>individual</i> mages, but they lack the cohesiveness to be dangerous as a faction. <br /> <br /> Personally, I go with the second one. I don't need the Nephandi to be anything more than a rot that's eating away at the innards of the Traditions, Technocracy, and Disparates. No grand conspiracy of corruptors, plotting and coordinating in concert; just the disconcertingly common tendency of mages to succumb to their wiles.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T05:26:13-07:00","id":860979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=860711#post860711\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This plan seems to require a ratio of 4:1 Nephandi:target mage to work. As well as a massive series of happy accidents to take the place of actual teamwork. That doesn't seem to argue that Nephandi are effective. It seems to argue that they might, in very rare, circumstances, be able to throw massive resources at a problem and, if everything doesn't go completely wrong, achieve a point where there's a reasonable chance they might be effective.</div> </div> </div> </div>Since Cheesefondue was saying that the different tactics of the Nephandi interfere with one another, I figured I would take the extreme example of using all four of the ones listed to show that isn't necessarily true. It was definitely an extreme example, but I can easily cobble together a similar kind of account using only two Nephandi. The point is that unless the Nephandi involved have very specific goals in mind, they aren't often at cross purposes.<br /> <br /> So no, the scenario doesn't rely on happy accidents unless you meant that in the Bob Ross sense. The idea isn't that everything has to go right, the idea is that even the things that go wrong can be turned into advantages by an enterprising Nephandus. If the assaulter gets the jump on Harvey before the others can act, Harvey is out of the way. If the rumors about Harvey kill his political sway, Harvey is out of the way. If Harvey reveals one of the Nephandi and leads the hunt against them, Harvey is out of the way (for the other three anyways). It's not that there's a narrow path of lucky coincidence the Nephandi can navigate to victory, it's that as long as their win conditions are open and general there's only a narrow path to true defeat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=860711#post860711\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again. &quot;So many irons in the fire&quot; implies LOTS and LOTS of Nephandi. To have all the irons required to have even the appearance of omnipresent plotting verging on imminent success from the Nephandi chapter, there would NEED to be that a high ratio of Nephandi to other mages.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or it means many plans. That's what I meant by it anyways. This goes back to that Nephandi propaganda I was talking about earlier. If every piece of corruption, destruction, or degradation is a blow for the Absolute, then they can claim victory in almost all circumstances. Corrupting a great leader and killing an incorruptible leader are both victories. Even failing at both and having to sacrifice pawns to evade the purge she leads against they is a victory because they've shown the world that the evil that was already in the system.<br /> <br /> It doesn't actually bring them closer to their true goals of course, but it gives them that horrible inevitable feeling they seem to enjoy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Also, I'm not defending the Nephandi chapter. I've already given my problems with it and how I think it should be used. If you want to try putting together a hypothetical 'Nephandi on the verge of victory scenario' and see how many it takes, though, I'm game.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T05:40:54-07:00","id":860988,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=860603#post860603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd argue that it isn't, or at least it shouldn't be. It's true for Nephandi that are complete nihilists and want to feed everything to Oblivion. And I suppose Nephandi who serve/worship the Maeljin Incarna want the universe to eventually be negated entirely after it's fully corrupted into a decayed morass, so same goal with different journey to get there. But infernalists serve the will of demons who are pretty clearly invested in continuing to exist, just with themselves in charge of the system rather than being defeated prisoners. And it's a flip of the coin whether the things in the Outer Dark want to be let into creation so they can feed off it and remake it in their own image or just snuff out existence as an affront to their alien sensibilities\u2014their worshippers clearly don't have enough understanding of their masters to definitively say one way or another. I think the attempt to unify all Nephandi under a single uniform and uniformly understood philosophy of nihilistic Descension is a big mistake.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Nephandi is not a catch all term for mages who do evil. Those who have been through the Cauls are metaphysically altered and they are ultimately working for Descent. How that manifests and what the Nephandi or their 'masters' think about it can vary wildly, but it's there nonetheless. <br /> <br /> So yeah, some are moving quickly and some are taking their time, but they are all working towards their own destruction.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T10:39:39-07:00","id":861143,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=860979#post860979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Since Cheesefondue was saying that the different tactics of the Nephandi interfere with one another, I figured I would take the extreme example of using all four of the ones listed to show that isn't necessarily true. It was definitely an extreme example, but I can easily cobble together a similar kind of account using only two Nephandi. The point is that unless the Nephandi involved have very specific goals in mind, they aren't often at cross purposes.<br /> <br /> So no, the scenario doesn't rely on happy accidents unless you meant that in the Bob Ross sense. The idea isn't that everything has to go right, the idea is that even the things that go wrong can be turned into advantages by an enterprising Nephandus. If the assaulter gets the jump on Harvey before the others can act, Harvey is out of the way. If the rumors about Harvey kill his political sway, Harvey is out of the way. If Harvey reveals one of the Nephandi and leads the hunt against them, Harvey is out of the way (for the other three anyways). It's not that there's a narrow path of lucky coincidence the Nephandi can navigate to victory, it's that as long as their win conditions are open and general there's only a narrow path to true defeat.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Or it means many plans. That's what I meant by it anyways. This goes back to that Nephandi propaganda I was talking about earlier. If every piece of corruption, destruction, or degradation is a blow for the Absolute, then they can claim victory in almost all circumstances. Corrupting a great leader and killing an incorruptible leader are both victories. Even failing at both and having to sacrifice pawns to evade the purge she leads against they is a victory because they've shown the world that the evil that was already in the system.<br /> <br /> It doesn't actually bring them closer to their true goals of course, but it gives them that horrible inevitable feeling they seem to enjoy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Also, I'm not defending the Nephandi chapter. I've already given my problems with it and how I think it should be used. If you want to try putting together a hypothetical 'Nephandi on the verge of victory scenario' and see how many it takes, though, I'm game.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's all for the sake of thrashing it out, as far as I'm concerned. I love it when someone gives me a viewpoint I'd never considered in one of these things and I get to rework things in my head. Not that that is happening for the Nephandi. I think the whole presentation smacks of apocalyptic hysteria cum paranoia over some relatively nasty people who just really aren't all that effective since the continued existence of Existence proves they've never ever gotten it right (for whatever reason... whether they work together like a well-oiled damnation machine or a hot mess). Are they scarey? Sure. But, bad guy/opponent/boogie-man scarey. Not end the universe scarey. Since they have never really made any headway on that for all of eternity. If they were as effective as they'd have to be the world would be dead a dozen times over. Gilles de Rais should have done the whole thing in. If not him, then the Nazis.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T13:41:06-07:00","id":861261,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=860472#post860472\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If four different Nephandi, an assaulter, a manipulator, a rumor monger, and a corrupter, all decide that a mage (let's call him Harvey) is in the way of their plans and get to work, what happens?</div> </div> </div> </div>I could create a story where they absolutely destroy each other's efforts (the manipulator vs the rumour monger especially - the manipulator wants Harvey in place but corrupted, the rumour monger wants Harvey kicked out because of rumours). But I think it's more effective to point at organisations in real life - &quot;everyone was working at cross purposes but it all still went well&quot; is not a common phrase in any circumstance (yes, there are very narrow regimented situations where that works - eg market competition - but you need a very stable underlying setup to get them).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4851-cheesefondue","name":"Cheesefondue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"275"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T05:34:09-07:00","id":861760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cheesefondue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=861261#post861261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I could create a story where they absolutely destroy each other's efforts (the manipulator vs the rumour monger especially - the manipulator wants Harvey in place but corrupted, the rumour monger wants Harvey kicked out because of rumours). But I think it's more effective to point at organisations in real life - &quot;everyone was working at cross purposes but it all still went well&quot; is not a common phrase in any circumstance (yes, there are very narrow regimented situations where that works - eg market competition - but you need a very stable underlying setup to get them).</div> </div> </div> </div>Look what you had to do to make that conflict happen, though. The manipulator needed Harvey to be X, the rumor monger needed Harvey to by Y. That's not their tactics being at cross purposes, it's their goals that conflict. And yes, that happens too, but it's a different underlying problem entirely and it's considerably rarer. <br /> <br /> The importance of coordination rises in direct proportion to the complexity of the goals. The Nephandi's goals, as a group, are about as general as you can get: show the world to be corrupt and dying, and whittle it down to nothing. It's not an accident that they are organized like loose rebellions, terrorist groups, and deep cover intelligence agencies. Those are groups that need to maximize effectiveness while minimizing coordination.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=861143#post861143\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's all for the sake of thrashing it out, as far as I'm concerned. I love it when someone gives me a viewpoint I'd never considered in one of these things and I get to rework things in my head. Not that that is happening for the Nephandi. I think the whole presentation smacks of apocalyptic hysteria cum paranoia over some relatively nasty people who just really aren't all that effective since the continued existence of Existence proves they've never ever gotten it right (for whatever reason... whether they work together like a well-oiled damnation machine or a hot mess). Are they scarey? Sure. But, bad guy/opponent/boogie-man scarey. Not end the universe scarey. Since they have never really made any headway on that for all of eternity. If they were as effective as they'd have to be the world would be dead a dozen times over. Gilles de Rais should have done the whole thing in. If not him, then the Nazis.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's my general thought on them as well. I do think that they are dangerous enough that they could have won already if everyone else hadn't stopped them. But secretly pulling the strings of everyone else? No. Their big problem is towards the end of whatever plans they have they have to get very obvious. The Technocracy can get away with that, because they carry the whispers and temptations of a better world. The Nephandi have made little to no headway in convincing people that they 'aren't that bad, really.'<br /> <br /> Although to be fair, destroying the world is a Mass Ascension level gambit, and to the best of our knowledge no one has managed one of those yet.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T15:39:57-07:00","id":862219,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi?p=860988#post860988\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Nephandi is not a catch all term for mages who do evil. Those who have been through the Cauls are metaphysically altered and they are ultimately working for Descent. How that manifests and what the Nephandi or their 'masters' think about it can vary wildly, but it's there nonetheless.<br /> <br /> So yeah, some are moving quickly and some are taking their time, but they are all working towards their own destruction.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is, however, a term for at least three different factions of evil mages who've mortgaged their souls/been remade by different masters and thus should have different ultimate goals whose main common characteristic is &quot;evil with a capital E.&quot; Hell's win condition is not the same as that of Malfeas, which is (probably) not the same as that of the Outer Dark, which may in fact have multiple competing agendas as varied as the sanity-blasting horrors that lurk in it. That setup continued to be the case from First Edition through Revised, and I'm not aware of it changing with M20. There's always been a contradiction between the organizing structures of the Nephandi and the unifying principle developers have been assigning to them since 2nd Edition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":